## Colorectal Polyps

- **Definition** - protuberance into lumen above the surrounding colonic mucosa
- **Classification of colonic polyps:**
  - **Non-neoplastic polyps:**
    - <u>Harmatomatous polyps</u> - polyps with mucosal cells but growing in a disorganised mass
    - <u>Hyperplastic polyps</u> - characteristically serrated polyps with proliferative characteristics and no cellular dysplasia
    - <u>Inflammatory polyps</u> - intraluminal protection of mucosal consisting of stromal, epithelial components rich in inflammatory cells
  - **Neoplastic polyps** = adenomatous polyps, sessile serrated polyps and traditional serrated adenomas
- **Clinical features:**
  - <u>Asymptomatic</u> - most are asymptomatic with incidental finding on routine screening or cross-sectional imaging of other intent
  - <u>PR bleeding or anaemia</u> - occurs in large polyps
  - <u>Tenesmus</u> - if polyp in rectum
  - <u>Intestinal obstruction</u> - for large polyps
- **Epidemiology** - 2/3 of all colonic polyps are adenomas:
  - <u>Demographic</u> - increasing incidence w/ age (25-30% by 50y), male preponderance, african-americans
- **Risk factors of adenomatous polyps:**
- **Endoscopic classification of polyps** - Paris Classification:
  - <u>Sessile</u> - polypoid lesions where the base and top of the lesion have the same diameter (granular mucosa is a feature of benign tumour)
  - <u>Pedunculated</u> - polypoid lesion with a narrow base resulting in a mucosal stalk interposed between the polyp and the wall
  - <u>Flat</u> - polypoidal lesions with height \< 50% of diameter of lesion
  - <u>Depressed</u> - lesions where entire thickness of mucosa in the lesion is less than that of the adjacent mucosa

  ![](img/comparison_of_3_types_of_ulcers.png) 
  ![](img/paris_classification_diagram.png)
- **Endoscopic features suggestive of invasive cancer:**
  - <u>Friability</u>
  - <u>Induration</u>
  - <u>Ulceration</u>
- **Histological classification of adenomatous polyps:**
  - **By architecture:**
    - <u>Tubular adenoma</u> (80%) - adenoma with \>= 75% tubular component with long branching adenomatous epithelium organised in tubules
    - <u>Villous adenoma</u> (5-15%) - adenoma with \>= 75% villous component with glands that are long and extend straight down from the surface to the center of the polyp
    - <u>Tubulovillous adenoma</u> (5-15%) - adenoma with 25-75% villous features
  - **By differentiation:**
    - <u>Low-grade dysplasia</u> - low risk of malignant transformation
    - <u>High-grade dysplasia</u> (intraepithelial carcinoma) - intermmediate step in progression from low-grade dysplasia and invasive cancer
- **Features of advanced adenomas** - high risk of metachronous CRC or polyp:
  - <u>Large polyps</u> - \> 10mm or \> 20mm
  - <u>Villous architecture</u> - villous components have higher risk of malignant transformation
  - <u>Poor differentiation</u> - high-grade dysplasia
  - <u>Multiple polyps</u>
- **Mx:**
  - **Principles of Mx:**
    - <u>Endoscopic removal</u> - indicated for all polyps
    - <u>Surveillance</u> - closer surveillance for selected patients after dependent on whether there is advanced adenomas
    - <u>Segmental colectomy</u> - presence of malignancy w/ risk factors of residual cancer and/or nodal metastasis
  - **Endoscopic removal:**
    - **Polypectomy** - complete removal of small adenomas using biopsy forceps
    - **Endoscopic resection techniques** (EMR, ESD) - reserved for large, sessile polyps
  - **Surveillance** - risk assessment for subsequent metachronous tumour or polyp, based on size, number, histology and differentiation:
    - **Advanced adenomas:**
      - <u>First surveillance</u> - at 3y (or 6mo if large polyps requiring piecemeal resection)
      - <u>Subsequent resection</u> - q3-5y based on 1st surveillance findings (shorter intervals for higher risk patients, e.g. advanced adenomas, or increased number of non-advanced adenomas)
    - **Non-advanced adenomas:**
      - <u>First surveillance</u> - q7-10y
      - <u>Subsequent surveillance</u> - interval depends on number of adenomas developed, and any hereditary risk of CRC
  - **Segmental colectomy** - for early CRC with high risk of residual disease or nodal metastasis:
    - <u>Indications</u> - malignant polyps (T1, i.e. invasion through muscularis mucosa) with certain features:
      - **Resection margins** - cancer at resection margins
      - **Submucosal invasion depth** - \> 1mm
      - **Differentiation** - poorly differentiated histology
      - **Evidence of metastasis** - lymphovascular invasion, perineural invasion, or tumour budding (foci of isolated cancer cells at the invasive margin of the polyp)

## Colorectal Cancer

### Epidemiology and risk factors

- **Epidemiology** - a common disease:
  - <u>Incidence</u> - highest incidence in western worlds
  - <u>Demographic</u> - uncommon \< 40y in patients for sporadic CRC, but incidence increases significantly after 40y
  - <u>Mortality</u> - declined progressively since mid-1980s due to earlier detection (e.g. screening), and better primary and adjuvant therapies
- **Early onset colorectal cancer** - global epidemiological trend towards early onset colorectal cancer (EOCRC), i.e. CRC diagnosed in patients \< (20% of CRC in the US):
  - **Risk factors:**
    - <u>Hereditary CRC syndromes</u> - up to 35% of young EOCRC in the West a.w hereditary CRC syndromes (necessitating genetic counseling and testing)
    - <u>Metabolic dysregulation</u> - obesity, metabolic syndrome, metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease etc.
    - <u>FHx</u> - Hx of CRC in 1st-degree relative
    - <u>Others</u> - alcohol, lack of NSAID use, inflammatory bowel disease, CKD, occupational exposure

### Pathology and prognostic determinents

- **Principles and prognostic determinants of colorectal cancer:**
  - <u>Early colorectal cancer</u> (Stage I through III) - determined by pathological staging and other clinical, histological and molecular features
  - <u>Metastatic colorectal cancer</u> (Stage IV) - determined by location and extent of metastasis (a/w good prognosis if resectable tumour)
- **Pathology of CRC** - evolution into different morphology despite similar adenoma-carcinoma sequence:
  - <u>Annulus lesions</u> (apple-core) - typically involve the left colon resulting in symptoms of bowel dysfunction (e.g. constipation, diarrhoea, or bowel obstruction)
  - <u>Polypoid lesions</u> (fungating exophytic mass) - typically grows large in the right colon often manifestating as unexplained Fe-deficiency anaemia
- **Histopathology of CRC** - \> 90% are adenocarcinomas with different subtypes bearing prognostic significance: 
![](img/who_classification_of_histopathology_of_crc.png)
  - **Mucinous AD** (11-17% of CRC) - copious extracellular mucin (\> 50% of tumour mass) have a predilection to right-sided CRC and may have poor responsiveness to upfront neoadjuvant chemoradiation and adjuvant chemotherapy (controversial)
  - **Signet cell AD** (1-2% of CRC) - characterised by intracellular mucin displacing nuclei, and is an aggressive tumour with propensity of intramural spread and peritoneal carcinomatosis (most present at stage III-IV) +/- a/w <u>microsatellite instability</u>
- **Histological grading of CRC** - degree of differentiation based on resemblence to glandular intestinal epithelium:
  - <u>Principles of histological grading</u> - area of resemblence to normal glandular intestinal epithelium based on cell-cell cooperation, maintenance of polarization, and coordinated secretion of cell products into cellular lumen +/- cytological features (e.g. mitotic rate, N:C ratio, atypia, pleomorphism)
  - <u>Histological grade</u> - 4 tiered grading system proposed by AJCC collapsed into 2-tiered system proposed by WHO as it retains prognostic power:
    - **High-grade tumour** - Well-to-moderately differented
    - **Low-grade tumour** - undifferentiated-to-poorly differentiated

### Pattern of spread and staging

- **Mode of spread of CRC:**
  - <u>Direct invasion</u> - longitudinal or transverse spread
  - <u>Lymphatic spread</u> - towards central LN <u>Haematogenous spread</u>:
    - **Liver** - typically 1st site of distant metastasis via portal system
    - **Lungs** - 2nd site of distant metastasis through pulmonary circulation (although may be initial site if rectal cancer due to drainage via inferior rectal vein)
    - **Bones**
    - **Brain**
  - <u>Transcoelomic spread</u> - through invasion through the serosa resulting in peritoneal carcinomatosis
- **Staging of CRC** - 8th edition AJCC TNM staging (Duke's classification no longer used):
  - **Clinical vs pathological staging:**
    - <u>Clinical staging</u> - depedent on radiographic, endoscopic, and intra-operative findings
    - <u>Pathological staging</u> - dependent on pathological and histopathological examination of the gross specimen after resection
  - **TNM stages:** 
  ![](img/tnm_staging_of_crc.png)
  - **Pronnostic stage groups:** 
  ![](img/prognostic_stage_groups.png)

### Prognostic determinants

### Clinical presentation of CRC

- **Asymptomatic** - present in individuals who undergo routine screening for CRC or as an incidental finding from an imaging study performed for another purpose
- **Sx of local tumour** - presenting Sx is often explained by tumour location:
  - **Abdominal pain** - can occur with tumours arising at all sites, and nature is dependent on pathological process:
    - <u>Partial obstruction</u> - often results in colicky peri-umbilical or lower abdominal pain (dependent on tumour location)
    - <u>Peritoneal carcinomatosis</u> - sharp, well-localised parietal pain
    - <u>Intestinal perforation</u> - sudden onset of severe, sharp well-localised parietal pain proceeding towards generalised peritonitis
  - **Sx related to left-sided CRC** - narrower lumen and solid faecal contents, such that it is more likely to present w/ obstructive symptoms:
    - <u>Obstructive symptoms</u>:
      - Colicky abdominal pain (40%) - caused by partial obstruction, especially with **annulus lesions**
      - Changes in bowel habits (74%) - alternating diarrhoea and constipation, changes in frequency and consistency of stool (e.g. bacterial liquefaction with stasis resulting in diarrhoea)
    - <u>PR bleeding</u> (51%) - usually caused by bleeding of a recto-sigmoid tumour
  - **Constitutional Sx** - weight loss, anorexia, malaise
  - **Sx related to right-sided tumour** - greater calibre and liquid faecal contents, resulting in less obstructive Sx and enable tumour to grow larger:
    - <u>Unexplained iron-deficiency anaemia</u> (10%) - caused by occult bleeding (right-sided tumours on average have 4x blood loss than left-sided tumours)
    - <u>Abdominal mass</u> (13%) - tumour grows large into a palpable or visible mass, especially with caecal tumours
  - **Sx of rectosigmoid tumours:**
    - <u>Haematochezia</u> - often slight in amount and at end of defaecation
    - <u>Altered bowel habits</u> - increased stool frequency with passage of small volume bloody mucus w/ little stool, classically in morning
    - <u>Deminished stool calibre</u> - i.e. pencil thin stools
    - <u>Tenesmus</u> - painful straining to empty bowel without resultant evacuation
    - <u>Obstructive Sx</u> - see above
- **Sx of metastatic disease** - dependent on site of metastasis:
  - **Liver** - RUQ distending pain, early satiety, hepatomegaly
  - **Lungs** - SOB with pleural effusion, cough, haemoptysis
  - **Peritoneum** - parietal pain (peritoneal carcinomatosis), abdominal distension (malignant ascites), periumbilical (Sister Mary Joseph) nodules
- **Atypical presentations:**
  - <u>Pyrexia of unknown origin</u> - usually caused by abscess from concealed perforations with underlying colonic malignancies
  - <u>Incidental finding of liver mass</u> - detected liver metastasis incidentally during USG studies, CT scans or on physical examination
  - <u>Malignant fistulations</u> - to bladder (pneumaturia) or SB
  - <u>Adenocarcinoma of unknown sites</u> - CRC accounts for site of origin of 6% of such cases
- **Presentation w/ complication** - large bowel obstruction, perforation, or acute PR bleeding:
  - <u>Intestinal obstruction</u> (20%) - urgent presentation with obstipation, abdominal pain and distension, and is a/w poor prognosis idependent of stage
  - <u>Intestinal perforation</u> - a complication of IO
  - <u>Acute PR bleeding</u> - exceedingly rare clinical manifestation

### Ivestigations and diagnostic evaluation

- **Ix and Dx evaluation** - histopathological diagnosis based on Bx obtained during colonoscopy:
  - **Endoscopy** - Colonoscopy preferred over sigmoidoscopy and allows Bx:
    - **Colonoscopy** - visualisation up to IC valve and AO for assessment of primary tumour, synchronous polyp or synchronous CRC
      - <u>Findings</u> - most are polypoidal lesions, while a minority are non-polypoidal, flat, or even depressed
    - **Sigmoidoscopy** - no role in assessment of CRC as the primary lesion may be right-sided, or there may be presence of synchronous tumours
  - **CT colonography** (virtual colonoscopy) - non-invasive method of diagnosis of polypoidal lesions after mechanical bowel preparation
    - <u>Indications</u> - incomplete colonoscopy
    - <u>Limitations</u>:
      - May miss smaller lesions
      - Non-diagnostic - colonoscopy required to obtain Bx for histological diagnosis
  - **Tumour markers** (e.g. CEA, CA19-9) - no diagnostic role but has prognostic significance and implications for assessment of Tx responsiveness and recurrence
- **DDx of CRC** - DDx of colonic mass seen on imaging or endoscopy:
  - <u>Lymphoma</u> - esp. primary NHL of the large bowel
  - <u>Other GI tumours</u> - GIST, neuroendocrine tumours
  - <u>Metastasis from other primary tumour</u> - e.g. ovarian cancer
  - <u>Kaposi sarcoma</u> - related to AIDS

### Staging Investigations

- **Staging Ix** - assessment for distant metastasis:
  - **Contrast CT T+A+P** - assessment of regional tumour extension, regional lymphatic metastasis, distant metastasis and tumour-related complications:
    - <u>Test properties</u> - higher Sn for detecting distant metastasis (75-87%) than nodal involvement (45-73%), transmural invasion (50%) or peritoneal seeding (\< 11% for \<0.5cm nodules, 37% for 0.5-5cm nodules)
    - 
  - **Liver MRI** - identify more hepatic lesions that are visualised by CT and is particularly valuable for patients w/ background of fatty liver changes
  - **PET-CT** - no evidence for routine pre-operative imaging for CRC as it <u>does not appear to add significant information</u>:
    - <u>Indications</u> - indicated for patients with suspected recurrence, or those with distant metastasis considering curative resection:
      - **Suspected recurrence** - for localising disease in patients with rising CEA levels and non-diagnostic conventional imaging evaluation following primary resection
      - **Evaluation of distant metastasis** - increased clinical information guiding surgical Mx of known distant metastasis (change in surgical Mx in 8% of patients)
    - <u>Tracers</u> - 18-FDG
  - **Ix for locoregional staging for rectal cancer** - for determining necessity of neoadjuvant therapy:
    - **Pelvic MRI** - Contrast pelvic MRI under DWI and T1-weighted images often acquired:
      - <u>Rationale</u> - good soft tissue contrast, enabling assessment of 1) tumour infiltration into muscularis propria and mesorectal fascia (T stage), and 2) nodal basins of the pelvis and lower abdomen (N stage)

      
      ![](img/mri_of_advanced_t3_rectal_cancer.png)
    - **Transrectal EUS** - limited utility for pretreatment staging of rectal cancer unless MRI contraindicated (e.g. pacemaker)

### Management of primary colon cancer

- **Principles of Mx:**
  - **Mx of localized disease:**
    - <u>Surgical Tx</u> - **surgical resection** of the tumour, the major vascular pedicles and lymphatic drainage segments +/- multivisceral resection with primary anastamosis (or diverting colostomy in special conditions)
    - <u>Neoadjuvant therapy</u> - no consensus for role of neoadjuvant therapy for primary colon cancer, but are contemplated in bulky nodal disease or clinically T4b disease
    - <u>Adjuvant chemotherapy</u> - indicated for stage III disease and selected high-risk stage II disease
    - <u>Adjuvant radiotherapy</u> - limited evidence of role of adjuvant RT, but considered for patients with high risk of local recurrence (e.g. T4 disease with penetration to fixed structure, positive resection margins, complicated presentation such as perforation or abscess formation)
  - **Mx of metastatic disease:**
    - <u>Surgical Tx</u> - 1) curative for selected patients with limited metastatic disease to liver and lung, or 2) can be performed for palliative reasons
    - <u>Systemic chemotherapy</u>
- **Surgical Mx** - surgical resection is the only curative modality for localised colon cancer and selected metastatic disease:
  - **Cadidates for resection** - C/I for assymptomatic patients with unresectable metastatic disease:
    - 1\) Localised CRC and selected patients with limited resectable metastatic disease (liver and lungs)
    - 2\) Palliation for patients with unresectable metastatic disease if symptomatic or complications occur (e.g. obstruction, perforation)
  - **Goals of surgical resection** - potentially curative resection:
    - 1\. En bloc removal of tumour with proximal and distal resection margins (5-7cm from tumour)
    - 2\. Removal of contiguous structures that are attached to or invaded by the tumour (i.e. multivisceral resection)
    - 3\. Resection of vascular pedicles and LN drainage basins (\> 12 LN resected for prognostic staging and guiding post-operative chemotherapy) of the ressected colonic segment
  - **Technical details:**
    - **Approach** - laparoscopic colectomy a/w faster recovery w/ no detrimental impact on oncological outcomes:
      - <u>Open approach</u> - complicated colon cancers typically require open approach
      - <u>Laparoscopic colectomy</u> - non-inferior to open colectomy and is performed in most uncomplicated case given expertise is present
    - **Extent of resection** - dependent on type and site of lesion:
      - **Malignant polyp** - endoscopic Tx for low-risk malignant polyp, but radical resection for those w/ high risk features (e.g. resection margin involved, submucosal invasion etc.)
      - **Localised cancers** - multivisceral resection required if locally advanced cancer invades or attaches to contiguous structures:
        - <u>Caecal or right colon</u> - right hemicolectomy
        - <u>Transverse colon</u> - extended right or left hemicolectomy depending which side of midline tumour resides in (or transverse colectomy if true midtransverse cancers)
        - <u>Left colon</u> - left hemicolectomy
        - <u>Sigmoid colon</u> - sigmoid colectomy with adequate margins
      - **Synchronous colon cancer** - two separate resections or one extended resection (e.g. subtotal or total colectomy of sporadic tumours on both sides)
  - **Restoration of bowel continuity** - primary anastamosis or temporary diverting ostomy:
    - <u>Primary anastamosis</u> - reserved for most patients w/ uncomplicated localised disease
    - <u>Temporary ostomy</u> - temporary diverting colonostomy or ileostomy performed if disease presents as complications (e.g. obstruction, perforation, acute PR bleeding)
  - **Resectable metastatic disease** (e.g. limited liver or lung metastasis) - surgical resection at primary site and metastatic site
- **Neoadjuvant chemotherapy** - no high-quality evidence supporting use of neoadjuvant therapy over upfront surgery (cf neoadjuvant chemotherapy is standard of care for <u>locally advanced rectal cancer</u>)
- **Adjuvant chemotherapy** - to eradicate micrometastasis and hence likelihood of disease recurrence:
  - <u>Indications</u>:
    - **Stage III disease** - i.e. all N1+ diseases receiving adjuvant chemotherapy is a/w 30% reduction in recurrence and 22-32% reduction in mortality
    - **Selected Stage II disease** - <u>considered</u> in stage II disease with features a/w high rate of recurrence (no high-level evidence for survival benefits):
      - <u>Pathological features</u> - T4 disease, poorly differentiated histology, perineural and lymphovascular invasion
      - <u>Clinical features</u> - presentation as complicated colorectal cancer (e.g. obstruction, perforation)
      - <u>Resected specimen</u> - \< 12 LN retrieved from resected specimen
  - <u>Regimen</u> - oxaliplatin-based therapy (FOLOX and short-term invusion 5-FU) is standard of care
- **Adjuvant RT** - lack of good evidence for role of RT in locally-advanced colon cancer (cf standard of practice for rectal cancers)
  - <u>Indications</u> - <u>considered</u> for patients w/ T4b diseases, +ve resection margins, or complicated cases w/ perforation and abscess formation

### Management of primary rectal cancer

- **Principles of Mx** - Tx selection dependent on tumour stage, distance between cancer from anal-verge:
  - **Mx of early rectal cancer** (T1-2N0/ Stage I):
    - <u>Surgical Tx</u>:
      - **Local excision** (selected T1 disease) - transanal excision, transanal endoscopc microsurgery (TEM) recommended for selected T1N0 tumours (malignant polyps that do not fit clinco-pathological criterias)
      - **Total mesocolic excision** (TME) - if criteria for local excision is not met
  - **Mx of locally-advanced rectal cancers** (T3-4 or N1+/ Stage II-III):
    - <u>Pre-operative RT and chemotherapy</u> - indicated for cT3-4 or N1+ diseases, or tumours invading circumferential margins
    - <u>Surgical Tx</u> - total mesocolic excision preferred:
      - **Spincter-preserving procedures** (e.g. LAR) - for upper and middle rectum disease (\> 5cm from anal verge) provided that -ve margins can be achieved
      - **Abdominoperineal resection** (APR) - for lower rectum disease (within 5cm from anal verge)
  - **Mx of metastatic disease** (Stage IV):
    - <u>Surgical Tx</u>:
      - **Currative Tx** (selected cases) - resection of primary tumour and metastatic site for those w/ resectable liver or lung disease
      - **Palliative Tx** - e.g. local or endoluminal Tx for palliation
    - <u>Palliative Tx</u> - chemo-irradiation
- **Surgical Tx:**
  - **Indications or candidates for excision or resection:**
    - 1\) Localised rectal cancer and selected patients with limited resectable metastatic disease (liver and lungs)
    - 2\) Palliation for patients with unresectable metastatic disease if symptomatic or complications occur (e.g. obstruction, perforation)
  - **Local excision** - reserved for selected early rectal cancers:
    - **Criteria of local excision** - selected T0-1 diseases, and can be theoretically offered to T2 disease:
      - <u>Local factors</u> - favourable T stage (T0-1, N0), tumour size (\< 3cm and \< 30% of bowel lumen circumference), mobile and non-fixed tumour, clear margins possible
      - <u>Histological factors</u> - low-grade tumour, absence of perineural or lymphovascular invasion
    - **Techniques** - transanal endoscopic microsurgery (TEM)
    - **Outcomes** - consistently poorer oncological outcomes esp. if a/w aforementioned criterias
  - **Low anterior resection** - sphincter-sparing resection for most local rectal tumours, preferred if <u>adequate distal margins can be achieved</u>:
    - **Indications and criteria for LAR** - 1) invasive rectal cancer (most cT1, cT2-4, N1+ disease), 2) Adequate distal margins (\>= 2cm)
    - **Outcomes** - lower recurrence rates (\< 10%) related to adequate margins, performing TME +/- adjuvant chemoradiation
  - **Abdominoperineal rection** - low-lying rectal cancers where a permanent diverting colstomy is used and anal opening is closed
- **Neoadjuvant chemoradiation** - indicated for locally advanced (T3-4/N1+) rectal cancer\*\* Hereditary Colorectal Cancer Syndrome

### Management of metastatic colorectal cancer

- **Treatment goals** - different according to clinical scenario:
  - <u>Potentially resectable disease</u> (esp. oligometastasis to the liver) - Tx of curative intent:
    - Upfront surgery for oligo-metastatic disease to the liver
    - Conversion chemotherapy for initially unresectable liver metastasis and subsequent surgical resection if adequate response to chemotherapy
  - <u>Non-resectable disease</u> - Tx of palliative intent by delaying tumour progression to prevent Sx development, prolong OS, and maintain QOL
- **Available agents in systemic therapy for mCRC:**
  - <u>Fluoropyrimidines</u> - including 5-fluorouracil (5-FU), levocovorin (LV), capecitabine
  - <u>Topoisomerase I inhibitor</u> - irinotecan
  - <u>Platinum-based chemotherapy</u> - oxaliplatin
  - <u>Anti-VEGF Ab</u> - Bevacizumab, Aflibercept
  - <u>EGFR inhibitor</u> - Cetuximab, panitumumab
  - <u>BRAF inhibitor</u> - encorafenib
  - <u>Multikinase inhibitor</u> - regorafenib
  - <u>Immune-checkpoint inhibitors</u> - Pembrolizumab
- **Predictive biomarkers** - molecular assessment (e.g. w/ NGS) of tumour tissue should be performed immediately after Dx of mCRC to guide Tx:
  - **dMMR/ MSI-H** - uniquely demonstrates susceptibility to immune-checkpoint inhibitors, and making <u>ICB a reasonable 1st-line Tx in appropriately selected patients</u>
  - **RAS** - differentiation of RAS wild-type (RASwt) and RAS mutated to <u>assess whether patient might benefit from EGFR inhibitors</u>:
    - **Types of RAS status:**
      - <u>Wild-type</u> - mutations in the KRAS and NRAS gene do not have constituitively active EGFR pathways and thus <u>may benefit from EGFR inhibitor</u>
      - <u>Mutant type</u> - RAS mutant (i.e. GOF mutation of KRAS/NRAS) have constituitively active EGFR pathways and thus <u>do not benefit from EGFR inhibitors</u>
    - **RAS testing:**
      - <u>Sample</u>:
        - Tissue biopsy from primary tumour or synchronous metastatic sites (gold standard)
        - Liquid biopsy from blood, CSF etc (currently evolving technique to improve turnaround time on mutation results)
        - LN (not applied)
      - <u>Principles of tests</u> - NGS to detect for mutant RAS in:
        - Exon 2 (codon 12, 13)
        - Exon 3 (codon 59, 61)
        - Exon 4 (codon 117, 146)
      - **Repeated testing** - required in recurrent tumour as <u>disconcordant results in RAS status was estimated at 20%</u>
    - **Implications on Mx** - RASwt are <u>eligible for EGFR inhibitors</u> (ceftuximab or panitumumab)
  - **BRAF** - specific BRAF V600E mutation <u>induces resistance against EGFR inhibitor</u> even if RASwt:
    - **Types of BRAF mutation:**
      - <u>V600E mutation</u> - this specific mutation in codon 600 of BRAF gene results in resistance against EGFR inhibitors, as documented by multiple meta-analysis and RCTs showing **no statistically significant effect of anti-EGFR on OS, PFS, and overall response rate**
      - <u>Wild-type V600 mutation</u> - better survival than V600E mutation (see below)
      - <u>Non-codon 600 mutation</u> - tends to show better median OS than V600E mutation and wt V600 mutation (61% vs 11% vs 43%), but response to EGFR inhibitors unsure
    - **Implications on Mx** - BRAF V600E mutation is unlikely to respond to EGFR inhibitor weather it is alone or in combination
  - **TMB-H** - high tumour mutational burden (TMB-H) is susceptible to ICB
  - **HER-2 status** - tumours with over-expressed HER-2 may respond to HER-targeted therapy which can be considered if the tumour progresses beyond initial therapy
- **Factors influencing selection of initial systemic therapy of mCRC:**
  - <u>Patient factors</u> - performance status, comorbidities, contraindications to bevacizumab
  - <u>Tumour factors</u>:
    - MSI-H - pembrolizumab mono-immunotherapy as first-line for MSI-H tumours irrespective of sidedness
    - RAS status - RASwt **may** benefit from anti-EGFR dependent on tumour sidedness
    - Tumour laterality - TKIs are reserved for left-sided CRCs w/ RASwt and BRAFwt, and are not prescribed for right-sided tumours (even if w/ RASwt and BRAFwt) as demonstrated absence of benefits or potential harm
- **Approach to selection of initial systemic therapy:**
  - <u>MSI-H tumours</u> - monoimmunotherapy (e.g. pembrolizumab) or nivolumab + ipilumab as acceptable alternative
  - <u>MSI-L tumours</u> - principally chemotherapy doublet (XELOX) with bevacizumab if not contraindicated/ anti-EGFR if indicated:
    - KRASwt and BRAFwt left-sided tumours - chemotherapy doublet + cetuximab/ panitumumab (chemotherapy backbone + bevacizumab also acceptable)
    - KRASwt and BRAFwt right-sided tumours - chemotherapy doublet plux bevacizumab (FOLFOXIRI +/- bevacizumab also acceptable)
    - KRAS mutant irespective of sideness - chemotherapy doublet plux bevacizumab (FOLFOXIRI +/- bevacizumab also acceptable)
    - RASwt and BRAF V600E mutant irrespective of sideness - FOLFOX + cetuximab and encorafenib

  
  ![](img/initial_systemic_therapy_for_mcrc.png)
- **Anti-EGFR MAb:**
  - <u>Selection</u> - cetuximab, panitumumab
  - <u>MOA</u> - binds to EGFR:
    - Competitive inhibition of growth factor signals
    - Stimulation of receptor dimerization and internalization
  - <u>S/E</u>:
    - Non-specific Sx - e.g. weakness, malaise
    - Cutaneous S/E - acneiform rash, pruritis
    - Hypomagnesemia (~20% Tx w/ cetuximab) - may result in secondary hypoK and hypoCa
    - Infusion reaction (more common w/ cetuximab)
